Yes, that's a good question. So hopefully it doesn't take too long to load this here, but let me go over to the repository for Archon

01:05:47.000 --> 01:05:51.000
Over to GitHub. All right. So the main thing is reliability.

01:05:51.000 --> 01:06:05.000
The problem with loops in Claude code or something like a Ralph loop, is you have no control over the actual workflow. Claude code itself defines the prompts for each iteration of the loop

01:06:05.000 --> 01:06:23.000
And so you really don't have much of a say of like what skills it's using, how it's validating things. I mean, you can try as much as you can to prompt it upfront to set the loop up in that way. But the beauty of Archon workflows is you're defining the process and you're making it as deterministic and repeatable as possible

01:06:23.000 --> 01:06:38.000
Because Archon allows you to define these workflows as YAML files where you set up the stages. This is how I want to plan, and then I want to go into another coding agent to take that plan and do the implementation, and then I want to run the tests

01:06:38.000 --> 01:06:49.000
But I want to do deterministically. Like, I'm not letting the LLM decide how to run the tests, because they are non-deterministic, they might get it wrong, or forget some of the tests that you tell it to run

01:06:49.000 --> 01:07:05.000
And so you're enforcing the test to be run in a certain way right after the implementation. And a lot of these problems of large language models being non-deterministic and forgetting to do things, they're exaggerated by loops, because loops are a longer set of work that you're laying out for it

01:07:05.000 --> 01:07:21.000
There's more for it to forget, and there's longer time in between you prompting it, so it's forgetting things that you're telling it to do in the system as a whole. Like, the orchestrator is going to lose track of your original goal over time. At least that's a risk that we have with loops in general

01:07:21.000 --> 01:07:32.000
So slash loop and quad code is cool because of how simple it is, but it's actually pretty limited in its reliability. And using slash loop in Claude code also, by the way, just keeps you in a single session.

01:07:32.000 --> 01:07:50.000
So it's a very token inefficient, and you start to get into that dumb zone for the large language model very quickly, because in between loops, it's not resetting the session, so you start to have, like, hundreds and hundreds of thousands of tokens in your context window, and the large language model gets completely overwhelmed with everything that you have. And so

01:07:50.000 --> 01:08:03.000
with our con workflows, we get to set up these independent nodes as separate coding agent sessions that are working together through handoff documents that we create. Like, handoffs, in general, are just a really big thing in the industry right now

01:08:03.000 --> 01:08:18.000
Because it's a way to keep your context lean and efficient, right? Like, you have one agent do the planning, and then it creates a plan document, and then you send that into another coding agent that does the implementation, and then it creates the implementation handoff that you then send into the review agent

01:08:18.000 --> 01:08:29.000
Right, like, we don't want to do everything all in one coding agent session, because we want to keep each individual agent as focused as possible. That's how we get the most reliable results. Archon helps you with that.

01:08:29.000 --> 01:08:45.000
And then also being able to even, like, do things in parallel. Like, my co-founder can look at a spec for North Star and decide, like, okay, I want to actually do 3 Archon workflows at the same time, because there are 3 features that I can easily build in parallel. Archon supports work trees under the hood

01:08:45.000 --> 01:09:06.000
So when you kick off an Archon workflow, it gets its own isolated environment, so that way it's not stepping on the toes of the other workflows that are running. Like, it has its own local copy of the code base, so it can work on its own thing and then create a pull request, and not worry about merge conflicts and tests breaking because the other coding agent was doing some edits as well

01:09:06.000 --> 01:09:09.000
We definitely want to avoid that kind of thing.

